{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-001-r1s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20260929-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Shared radix-4 combinational divider with parallel trial subtraction\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline has a 199.837 ns, 156-cell worst path through forwarding,\n  the execute-stage ALU, and the result register, and reaches only 5.442 MHz.\n  The ALU expresses signed and unsigned quotient and remainder with four\n  separate / and % operators. Deep combinational division is the strongest\n  candidate for this path. Reducing its serial logic depth should raise\n  CoreMark iterations/second without adding instruction cycles or changing\n  the existing memory-stall behavior. No earlier micro_opt attempts or\n  distilled lessons exist for this fixture beyond the baseline retest.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU arithmetic in alu.sv with one\n  shared, fully combinational unsigned radix-4 quotient/remainder network.\n  Normalize signed DIV/REM operands to 32-bit unsigned magnitudes; unsigned\n  operations use the original operands. Consume the dividend two bits at\n  a time, most significant pair first, in sixteen unrolled stages. Starting\n  with remainder R=0, each stage forms the 34-bit trial T={R,next_pair}.\n  Precompute zero-extended D, 2D, and 3D once with enough width to prevent\n  overflow. Compute T-D, T-2D, and T-3D in parallel as 35-bit unsigned\n  subtractions. Their borrow bits select the largest nonnegative candidate\n  and the corresponding quotient digit 1, 2, or 3; select T and digit 0\n  when all three borrow. Use mutually exclusive digit predicates and a\n  parallel masked-OR remainder mux rather than a cascaded priority data\n  mux. For nonzero D, every selected remainder is less than D and fits\n  32 bits. Concatenate the sixteen two-bit digits to obtain the quotient,\n  and use the final remainder directly, avoiding q*D remainder recovery.\n  Restore the quotient sign using the dividend/divisor sign XOR and the\n  remainder sign using the dividend sign. Explicit final overrides retain\n  division-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 quotient/remainder semantics.\n…[1124 of 4196 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d1dc475f80794a02]…\ne divider\n      locally in this existing source file, with explicitly widened divisor\n      multiples and borrow-producing subtraction, a shared quotient and\n      remainder, parallel one-hot candidate selection, signed restoration,\n      and exact RV32M exceptional outputs. Replace the four real division\n      operator arms only; preserve ALTOPS and all other ALU behavior.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every existing check and extend real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU vectors\n      with deterministic random signed/unsigned operands, every sign pairing,\n      zero divisors, INT_MIN/-1, high-bit divisors, and values immediately\n      below/equal/above divisor multiples. Check exact quotient/remainder\n      bits against independent integer arithmetic, and check the paired\n      identity and remainder bounds for nonzero, nonoverflow cases. Run the\n      existing pipeline cosim/formal gates and canonical stalled CoreMark\n      evaluation to verify unchanged retirement, CRCs, and measured cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
