{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 (improvement, +807.59%): Combinational `/` and `%` in EX constrain the clock for ordinary instructions too, so moving DIV/REM to a registered blocking iterative divider greatly improves CoreMark throughput when its timed kernels contain no division.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r2s0 (regression, -3.11%): Removing redirect-dependent instruction muxes and ID/EX payload clears exposed Gowin's BRAM register-file-to-EX forwarding/DSP multiply-to-dmem address path as the timing limit, so reduced control-path depth did not translate into higher median Fmax.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (improvement, +8.21%): With combinational IF/ID, a 64-entry PC-only tagged next-PC predictor saves taken-transfer fetch bubbles while parallel successor comparisons and ID/EX validity/side-effect kills keep recovery off decode and wide payload enables.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r3s0 (regression, +5.94%): Sharing RV32M modes through a signed 33x33 product fed directly by forwarded rs1/rs2 bypasses ALU operand muxes and cuts DSPs from six to two, but unchanged CoreMark cycles make the gain depend on final median placement Fmax, which fell from the prototype's 55.538 MHz to 52.930 MHz.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r3s1 (improvement, +8.80%): Capturing post-forwarding operands and registering the DSP product splits the register-file-to-dmem multiply path across clock intervals, letting the clock gain outweigh two added EX cycles per MUL in CoreMark's matrix kernels.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 (regression, +0.48%): Resolving load-dependent branches in MEM from registered WB operands removes load-use bubbles and saves 3.17% of CoreMark cycles, but median Gowin Fmax falls from 49.962 to 48.612 MHz, nearly cancelling the cycle savings and making recovery timing a constraint on speculative hazard removal.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260929-001-r4s0 (regression, -1.11%): Registering `predictor_update_t` table feedback preserved CoreMark cycles but left immediate redirect/fetch-to-BRAM `ADB` paths intact\n…[3995 of 7067 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:571f06fb0ca405b6]…\ncing a CoreMark throughput gain that still trails cycle-neutral ID/EX ALU operand preselection.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r9s0 (improvement, +6.68%): Grouping fixed-slice shifts into 0/1/2/3, 0/4/8/12 and 0/16 mask-and-OR stages and sharing SRL/SRA routing with op-gated original sign fill shortens ALU timing and reduces LUT4 use at unchanged CoreMark cycles, leaving forwarding and predictor paths as the next timing limits.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r9s1 (regression, -8.32%): Splitting SLL/SRL/SRA across EX/MEM and MEM/WB does not isolate forwarding-to-redirect timing, which became critical and reduced median Fmax from 87.839 to 81.036 MHz while shift-use interlocks added 0.63% CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r10s1 (regression, -12.03%): Precomputing `mismatch_sequential` and `mismatch_pc_target` in decode extends BTB tag lookup through successor equality into ID/EX, making that frontend path critical and lowering median Gowin Fmax from 93.708 to 82.434 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
