{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n --hidden -g '\"'!'\"\\\\.git/**' -g '\"'!'\"\\\\.codex-home/**' 'hyp-20260930-002-r9s1|192\\\\.02|209\\\\.45|4193876|4,193,876|shift_late_result' cores/bench\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260930-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Split execute effects into registered memory-address and recovery lanes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 180.46 iterations/second at 74.535 MHz and\\n4,130,235 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst Gowin path runs from\\nMEM/WB write_value through EX and the general ALU into the data-memory\\naddress input: 19.124 ns across 13 cells. Registered bypass choices\\nhave already removed producer matching and priority from EX, but\\nmemory addresses still traverse general operand/result muxes, while\\nresolved next-PC feedback drives fetch and predictor memories directly.\\nEarlier structural fetch/operand-stage experiments either added cycles\\nwithout raising Fmax or failed stalled cosim. Replacing register storage\\nalso left the same execution-to-memory limit. Preserve those lessons by\\nseparating execute's externally applied effects at its existing output\\nboundary, rather than adding a general operand stage.\\n\\nScratch measurements against the unchanged wrappers show why this must\\nbe one complete output-lane split. A dedicated address alone reached\\nonly 71.281 MHz median and exposed forwarding-to-frontend feedback;\\nregistered recovery alone reached only 69.288 MHz and retained the ALU\\npath into dmem. Separating and registering both effect lanes reached\\n80.738 MHz median, with the worst path now ending at an integer-result\\nregister instead of either memory endpoint. This proposal selects that\\nsingle execution-output topology, not either incomplete prototype.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion scores 180.46 iterations/second at 74.535 MHz and\\n4,130,235 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst Gowin path runs from\\nMEM/WB write_value through EX and the general ALU into the data-memory\\naddress input: 19.124 ns across 13 cells. Registered bypass choices\\nhave already removed producer matching and priority from EX, but\\nmemory addresses still traverse general operand/result muxes, while\\nresolved next-PC \n…[63275 of 66347 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0cc482b47c40db1a]…\n\": 6.68, \"vs_baseline\": 1743.56, \"fmax_mhz\": 93.708, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4193876, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3584, \"ff\": 1188, \"seeds\": [92.907, 93.708, 93.708], \"perturbations\": [[0, 92.907, 11], [1, 93.708, 10], [2, 93.708, 10]], \"lutram\": 32, \"bsram\": 7, \"dsp\": 2, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.write_value_14_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.fwd_rs2_sel_1_s0\", \"slack_ns\": -5.763, \"arrival_ns\": 16.058, \"logic_cells\": 25, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_fwd\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 11, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-30T04:33:47.314900Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r9s0 (improvement, +6.68%): Grouping fixed-slice shifts into 0/1/2/3, 0/4/8/12 and 0/16 mask-and-OR stages and sharing SRL/SRA routing with op-gated original sign fill shortens ALU timing and reduces LUT4 use at unchanged CoreMark cycles, leaving forwarding and predictor paths as the next timing limits.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
