{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n --hidden 'hyp-20260930-003-r9s2|215\\\\.56|3,?868,?725|84\\\\.758' cores/bench -g '\"'!.git/**'\"'\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260930-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Registered fetch tokens break the instruction-stream replay feedback path\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 192.51 at 80.738 MHz over 4,193,876 marker-bracketed\\nCoreMark cycles. Its 64-entry next-PC predictor already captures most\\nbranch-direction benefit. The prior eight-block stream predictor saved\\n9.33 percent of cycles on the preceding champion but reached only\\n64.584 MHz. Its worst path ran from replay ownership through IF and the\\nhazard unit back into a replay word-index register. Repeating that\\nfall-through replay frontend would recreate the demonstrated bottleneck.\\nRegistering fetched instruction tokens can separate replay availability\\nand data selection from decode hazards while retaining the measured\\ninstruction-stall savings. This is one stream-predictor hypothesis;\\nchanges to frontend buffering serve that predictor's timing boundary.\\n\\nA scratch prototype on the exact current champion uses 3,868,725\\nbracketed cycles with both standard pseudo-random bus stalls, a 7.75\\npercent reduction. The canonical five CRCs, validation literal,\\nEBREAK completion and OOB check all pass. Unchanged selftest and three\\nrandom-program RVFI cosim comparisons also pass with both bus stalls.\\nOn the corrected scalar-token RTL, vendor placement options 0/1/2\\nreport 80.111/82.513/82.513 MHz, giving a median of 82.513 MHz and\\napproximately 213.28 iterations/second, 10.79 percent above 192.51.\\nArea is 5,143 LUT4 and 1,684 FF, with 96 LUTRAM, seven BSRAM and two\\nDSP. This is prototype evidence, not an accepted tournament result.\\nThe unchanged isolated formal suite passed 52 of 53 checks;