g multicycle EX operations using one\nshared radix-2 restoring divider. Capture the post-forwarding operands\nand operation exactly once, register quotient/remainder state, and\nperform one 33-bit subtract/select step per cycle for 32 iterations.\nRegister sign correction and the final result before it returns to the\nordinary EX/MEM path. This removes every real combinational / and %\noperator from the synthesized ALU, while keeping RV32I and multiplication\nat their current latency.\n\nFreeze fetch and ID/EX while the divider owns EX, but let older EX/MEM\nand MEM/WB instructions drain. Insert invalid, side-effect-free EX/MEM\nbubbles while waiting; do not repeatedly hold and reissue an older\nmemory operation. Existing dmem backpressure still ho\n…[6083 of 9155 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ef3421dcffbb4dbc]…\nbe measured. With that cycle count, acceptance\nrequires median Fmax above approximately 55.8 MHz, since the Fmax ratio\ndivided by the measured cycle-count ratio must exceed 1.046. A 65 MHz\nclock would yield roughly 22 percent fitness gain under this estimate.\nPredict 20 percent with medium confidence: the removed feedback is the\nobserved worst path, but remaining multiplier, divider-step, register-\nfile and PC-update paths may become limiting. Validate actual cycle\ncount, canonical CRCs and vendor median timing; this is an unmeasured\narchitectural hypothesis, not a claim of achieved improvement.\n\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"PC-indexed next-PC prediction with a short recovery control path\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"fitness\": 119.03, \"fmax_mhz\": 49.962, \"cycles\": 4197447, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -15.386, \"arrival_ns\": 25.681, \"logic_cells\": 15, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
