{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'predictor|hyp-20260929-003-r1s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"64-entry tagged next-PC predictor with two-bit branch direction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline fetches sequentially and flushes the combinational IF/ID\\ninstruction whenever EX resolves a taken branch or jump. Because IF/ID\\nis not registered, each such transfer loses one fetch/decode opportunity,\\nrather than the two bubbles suggested by the five-stage comments.\\nCoreMark repeatedly executes loop branches and call sites, making these\\nbubbles a promising source of cycle savings. The measured 199.837 ns\\ncritical path is through forwarding and the combinational ALU; a small\\nPC-indexed predictor should improve iterations/cycle without adding\\nlogic to that arithmetic path. No predictor experiments or lessons are\\npresent in this fixture's history.\\n\\nA fresh architectural trace of the canonical -O3, RV32IM, 2K,\\nten-iteration CoreMark ELF contains 2,922,016 bracketed instructions,\\n529,588 conditional branches, 49,367 JALs, and 13,735 JALRs. Sequential\\nprediction would mispredict 357,527 next PCs. A 64-entry tagged table\\nwith this policy reduces next-PC errors to 62,551; 32 entries give\\n73,619 errors and 128 entries give 58,167. Thus 64 entries capture most\\nof the available benefit without doubling storage for only 4,384 fewer\\nerrors. This trace model uses immediate training and no speculative\\npipeline or bus timing, so it supports the sizing decision rather than\\nconstituting a measured RTL speedup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline fetches sequentially and flushes the combinational IF/ID\\ninstruction whenever EX resolves a taken branch or jump. Because IF/ID\\nis not registered, each such transfer loses one fetch/decode opportunity,\\nrather than the two bubbles suggested by the five-stage comments.\\nCoreMark repeatedly executes loop branches and call sites, making these\\nbubbles a promising source of cycle savings. The measured 199.837 ns\\ncritical path is through forwarding and the combinational ALU; a small\\nPC-indexed predi\n…[8194 of 11266 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b6e8b31a81bbbddf]…\ns,\\nchanging JALR targets, back-to-back predicted transfers, alignment\\ntraps, and a trained PC reused by a non-control instruction. Exercise\\nimem and prolonged dmem stalls during prediction and recovery; assert\\nexact retirement PCs/orders and no wrong-path register/store effects.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 13.07, \"delta_pct\": 7.84, \"vs_baseline\": 7.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.487, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4197517, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 13424, \"ff\": 685, \"seeds\": [5.132, 5.487, 5.487], \"perturbations\": [[0, 5.132, 147], [1, 5.487, 149], [2, 5.487, 149]], \"lutram\": 96, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_1_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -189.85, \"arrival_ns\": 200.144, \"logic_cells\": 152, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 147, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-29T20:11:43.775078Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,210p' cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\nsed -n '1,185p' cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\nsed -n '1,140p' Makefile\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,210p' cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\nsed -n '1,185p' cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\nsed -n '1,140p' Makefile\"", "aggregated_output": "// rtl/if_stage.sv\n//\n// Instruction fetch stage. Holds the PC register; the IF/ID payload\n// (pc + instr + valid) is *combinational* — there is no separate IF/ID\n// flop in this microarchitecture, the next-stage's ID/EX register\n// captures everything one cycle later.\n//\n// On flush or redirect, the instruction emitted to ID is forced to NOP\n// (`0x00000013` = ADDI x0,x0,0). This prevents the hazard unit from\n// observing a real rs1/rs2 from a wrong-path instruction and inserting\n// a spurious load-use stall the cycle after a taken branch.\n//\n// Latency:        PC-reg update is synchronous; output is combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds pc_rdata (via ID/EX/MEM/WB) and pc_wdata (via\n//                 EX-stage redirect).\nmodule if_stage (\n  input  logic              clock,\n  input  logic              reset,\n  input  logic              stall,            // hold PC (load-use)\n  input  logic              flush,            // emit NOP into ID this cycle\n  input  logic              redirect,         // EX has resolved a branch/jump\n  input  logic [31:0]       redirect_target,\n  output logic [31:0]       imem_addr,\n  input  logic [31:0]       imem_data,\n  output if_id_t  out\n);\n\n  localparam logic [31:0] RESET_PC = 32'h0000_0000;\n\n  logic [31:0] pc;\n  logic [31:0] next_pc;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    next_pc = redirect ? redirect_target : pc + 32'd4;\n  end\n\n  // Redirect must override stall: a BRANCH/JAL/JALR in EX may fire\n  // redirect on the same cycle as imem_stall or dmem_stall — without\n  // this priority the redirect target would be silently dropped, the\n  // PC would hold its old (wrong-path) value, and execution would\n  // resume on the wrong path once the bus unstalls. Verified by the\n  // VexRiscv-binary CoreMark sweep with --istall enabled.\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if      (reset)    pc <= RESET_PC;\n    else if (redirect) pc <= redirect_target;\n    else if (!stall)   pc <= next_pc;\n  end\n\n  assign imem_addr = pc;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    out.pc    = pc;\n    out.instr = (flush || redirect) ? 32\n…[9000 of 12072 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3ba015e8a702b3a1]…\n/selftest.elf bench/programs/coremark.elf\n\tpython3 -m tools.eval.cosim . $(TARGET)\n\ncosim-build: $(wildcard $(RTL_DIR)/*.sv) test/cosim/main.cpp\n\tbash test/cosim/build.sh\n\nbench/programs/selftest.elf: bench/programs/selftest.S bench/programs/link.ld\n\t$(MAKE) -f bench/programs/Makefile bench/programs/selftest.elf\n\nformal:\n\tbash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks.cfg\n\n# formal-deep: same checks WITHOUT RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS so bitwuzla\n# evaluates the actual MUL/DIV/REM bitvector formulas. SLOW (each\n# M-ext check can take 15+ min wall). Use periodically — the\n# orchestrator's per-iteration formal gate uses fast `make formal`.\n# See formal/checks.cfg comments for the full ALTOPS scope explanation.\nformal-deep:\n\tbash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks-deep.cfg\n\nbench:\n\t$(MAKE) -f bench/programs/Makefile all\n\n# FPGA timing and area come from the vendor flow, Gowin EDA\n# (tools/eval/gowin.py); V2 does not synthesize with Yosys.\nfpga: cosim-build bench/programs/coremark.elf\n\tpython3 -m tools.eval.fpga . $(TARGET)\n\ntiming:\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
