{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git status --short; sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r3s2.yaml; rg -n 'pending|defer|stall|train|predict' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py | tail -70\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20260929-003-r3s2\ntitle: \"Predict through load-dependent branches with delayed registered resolution\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's 64-entry tagged next-PC predictor already removes\n  most control-transfer bubbles and scores 119.03 at 49.962 MHz over\n  4,197,447 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Merely enlarging its table has little\n  remaining headroom: the previous architectural model found only 62,551\n  next-PC errors at 64 entries versus 58,167 at 128. A fresh execution of\n  the canonical RV32IM, -O3, 2K, ten-iteration ELF finds 135,320 immediately\n  load-dependent conditional branches among 2,922,015 instructions strictly\n  between the marker stores. The hazard unit currently stalls these\n  branches even though fetch already knows their predicted successor.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the existing next-PC prediction to issue legal conditional branches\n  immediately behind a producer load without the usual load-use bubble.\n  Resolve only these dependent branches one stage later, from a registered\n  load result, while continuing fetch along their captured prediction.\n  Keep ordinary branches, jumps and all other load consumers on their\n  existing paths. This is one predictor-enabled speculative branch policy,\n  not an instruction-cache or arithmetic change.\n\n  At decode, validate the BRANCH opcode and funct3 before exempting an\n  actual rs1/rs2 dependence on a valid nonzero-destination EX load. Carry\n  explicit delayed-resolution metadata. In EX capture the branch PC,\n  prediction, target, operation, both forwarded operands and which source\n  operands need the immediately preceding load. Do not resolve or train\n  that branch using the load's effective address as operand data. When the\n  branch reaches MEM, substitute the preceding load's registered WB result\n  for each marked source, resolve the real branch condition and alignment\n  trap, and produce the architectural RVFI operands and successor. No\n  combinational dmem-data-to-EX comparison or ALU bypass is introduced.\n\n  Correct predict\n…[5328 of 8400 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8fe9bbf0d38980fe]…\nsert len(stalled_pcs) == 9 and len(set(stalled_pcs)) == 1\n744:async def predictor_warm_taken_loop_and_exit(dut):\n748:    for stalled in (False, True):\n753:            if not stalled:\n769:        assert [u[\"prediction\"] for u in branches] == [16] + [8] * 7\n771:        if stalled:\n779:async def predictor_alternating_direction(dut):\n804:async def predictor_back_to_back_transfers_and_tag_collisions(dut):\n839:async def predictor_changing_jalr_targets(dut):\n862:    assert [u[\"prediction\"] for u in jumps] == [16, 0x40, 0x60, 0x40]\n868:async def predictor_stale_code_alignment_and_prolonged_holds(dut):\n869:    # Reuse a trained JAL PC as arithmetic, illegal code, misaligned control,\n888:            trained = sum(u[\"pc\"] == 4 for u in monitor.updates)\n889:            if replacement == LW(6, 0, 0) and trained >= 3 and pc == 16:\n893:            return replacement if pc == 4 and trained >= 3 else original\n930:        assert len(updates) == 4, \"held EX trained repeatedly\"\n932:        assert updates[-1][\"prediction\"] == 16\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport struct,collections,json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=struct.iter_unpack('<4I',Path('cores/bench/generated/hypgen_profile/trace.bin').read_bytes())\nbtb=[None]*64; errors=collections.Counter(); dep_errors=collections.Counter(); last=None; dep_sites=collections.Counter()\nfor pc,insn,npc,rd in rows:\n op=insn&127; ix=(pc>>2)&63; old=btb[ix]\n predicted=old[1] if old and old[0]==pc and (old[2] or old[3]>=2) else (pc+4)&0xffffffff\n dep=last is not None and last[1]&127==3 and last[3] and last[3] in ((insn>>15)&31,(insn>>20)&31) and op==0x63\n if predicted\"'!=npc:\n  errors[hex(op)]+=1\n  if dep: dep_errors[hex(op)]+=1; dep_sites[pc]+=1\n if op in (0x63,0x67,0x6f):\n  if op==0x63:\n   imm=((insn>>31)<<12)|(((insn>>7)&1)<<11)|(((insn>>25)&63)<<5)|(((insn>>8)&15)<<1)\n   if imm&4096: imm-=8192\n   target=(pc+imm)&0xffffffff; taken=npc!=((pc+4)&0xffffffff)\n  else: target=npc; taken=True\n  count=old[3] if old and old[0]==pc else (1 if taken else 2)\n  count=min(3,count+1) if taken else max(0,count-1)\n  btb[ix]=(pc,target,op!=0x63,count)\n elif old and old[0]==pc: btb[ix]=None\n last=(pc,insn,npc,rd)\nprint('\"'instant-trained predictor errors',dict(errors),'sum',sum(errors.values()))\nprint('errors in adjacent load-dependent branches',dict(dep_errors),'sum',sum(dep_errors.values()))\nprint('sites',dep_sites.most_common(15))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
