{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_86", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r4s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20260929-003-r4s2\ntitle: \"Register predictor training before target-table writes\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 129.51 at 56.790 MHz over 4,384,920 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. Its recorded worst path is now 22.917 ns and 36 cells,\n  from cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0 to cpu/u_if/tags_tags_0_0_s, through ID,\n  EX and IF. Registering multiplication exposed predictor training as a\n  timing constraint: live forwarded EX operands still influence the\n  branch outcome, target alignment and enables used by the predictor's\n  synchronous table writes. The existing 64-entry predictor already\n  captures most useful next-PC prediction. Prior predictor experiments\n  found little benefit from doubling its capacity, while speculative\n  load-dependent branch resolution saved 3.17 percent of cycles but lost\n  enough Fmax to miss acceptance. Breaking the measured predictor-update\n  path is the strongest remaining predictor-specific opportunity.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert one registered, full-throughput training boundary between EX\n  resolution and the existing 64-entry tagged next-PC table. Capture the\n  complete predictor_update_t bundle (valid, allocate, unconditional,\n  taken, PC and resolved target) at every edge. Reset only its valid bit;\n  write its payload only for a valid advancing EX instruction. Consume\n  the captured update at the following edge using the existing allocation,\n  matching-entry invalidation and two-bit saturating-counter policy.\n  Table write address, data and enables must depend exclusively on this\n  registered bundle, registered table state and reset, never directly on\n  live EX operands, control_target, branch_cond, ex_wait or dmemReady.\n  This is a one-cycle delay of predictor training, with capacity for one\n  update each cycle; it must never serialize control transfers or stall EX.\n\n  Retain the current registered-PC-only asynchronous lookup, 64 entries,\n  full tags and aligned targets, conditional counters, unconditional bit,\n  combinational IF/ID topology and immed\n…[3390 of 6462 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:39fc96dc93ed6843]…\ne: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt training latency assertions to distinguish event capture from\n      table consumption. Check no table visibility at capture, visibility\n      after consumption, bubble clearing, consecutive same-PC saturation,\n      colliding tags, matching-only invalidation, changed JALR targets,\n      pre-edge lookup on concurrent writes, commit during stalls/redirects\n      and reset cancellation of a pending event.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise short warmed loops and back-to-back control transfers with\n      delayed training under imem and dmem stalls and blocking arithmetic.\n      Check exact retirement PCs and contiguous order, stale-target\n      recovery, no repeated training of held instructions, no wrong-path\n      stores or writers, and progress through reset and misalignment traps.\n      Preserve existing correctness gates and compare canonical stalled\n      CoreMark marker cycles and Gowin median fitness with the champion.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
