{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_46", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r6s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20260930-001-r6s0\ntitle: \"Precompute forwarding address matches in decode\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 150.32 iterations/second at median Gowin Fmax\n  62.085 MHz and 4,130,235 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst vendor\n  path starts at MEM/WB rd, crosses forwarding and EX, and ends at a\n  fetch-predictor RAM address. A fresh unchanged-core placement-option-0\n  build reproduces 59.030 MHz and the exact 22.235 ns, 44-cell path.\n  A nearly tied path ends at dmem through the ordinary ALU. Both begin\n  with late producer-destination equality, then high-fanout operand\n  steering before execution. The accepted banked register file already\n  improved RF timing; replacing it with flops did not beat this champion.\n  Prior micro_opt training-feedback retiming left immediate recovery\n  and ALU paths limiting timing. Move the address comparisons earlier\n  without adding a pipeline stage or changing producer priority.\nhypothesis: |\n  Apply one local micro-optimization: precompute and register the four\n  EX forwarding address matches at the existing ID/EX capture edge.\n  Keep the current two-bit forwarding selectors and EX operand muxes,\n  arithmetic, branch predictor, skid buffer and instruction latencies.\n  No extra stage, prediction policy or architectural state is introduced.\n\n  When ID accepts a fetch token, the current ID/EX producer advances\n  into EX/MEM and the current EX/MEM producer advances into MEM/WB.\n  Compare each incoming instr[19:15] and instr[24:20] against current\n  ID/EX.rd for next-cycle EX/MEM forwarding, and current EX/MEM.rd for\n  next-cycle MEM/WB forwarding. Include producer rd != 0 in each\n  comparison. Register these four Boolean results alongside ID/EX\n  using precisely its existing !stall payload enable. Reset them to\n  zero. They may be two independent two-bit output registers in\n  id_stage; core_pkg.sv and the existing packed payload need not change.\n\n  Replace forward_unit's live address equality/nonzero tests with these\n  registered matches. Continue to qua\n…[3658 of 6730 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:32aa6309cf15d578]…\n priority, default selection and the existing two-bit output encoding.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire ID/EX.rd and EX/MEM.rd to id_stage for advance-time matching,\n      and route its four registered match bits to forward_unit. Remove\n      unused live address inputs from the forwarding instance. Keep the\n      entire EX interface and top-level nret=1 memory/RVFI ports intact.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every existing assertion. Add cases with two older writers\n      to the same rd, both source ports, immediate-bit false dependencies,\n      x0, trapped producers and load consumers, then combine them with\n      prolonged dmem holds, imem outages, load-use bubbles and blocking\n      M completion followed immediately by a dependent consumer. Verify\n      exact RVFI sources/results/PCs, contiguous order and once-only\n      memory effects. Run unchanged formal, zero-wait/stalled random\n      trace cosim, canonical CRC/marker checks and median Gowin timing.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
