{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_55", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r7s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20260930-001-r7s0',\n'title':'Dedicated load/store address generation outside the generic ALU result mux',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion's registered one-hot bypass choices raised median Gowin Fmax\n to 74.535 MHz at 4,130,235 bracketed CoreMark cycles, but the remaining\n worst path still starts at MEM/WB.write_value[17], crosses EX and the\n general ALU, and ends at a data-memory BSRAM input: 19.124 ns and thirteen\n cells. The memory interface currently takes its effective address from\n the shared EX/MEM.alu_result bus, so arbitrary shift, compare, multiply\n completion and ALU-result selection circuitry can constrain the physical\n RAM address path even though loads/stores only require rs1 + immediate.\n Prior register-file and complete bypass-mask improvements make that\n unnecessary coupling the most promising remaining local micro_opt.\n'''.replace('\\\\n ','\\\\n'),\n'hypothesis':'''Specialize the existing EX/MEM memory-address datapath. Add a dedicated\n32-bit effective_addr register field computed in EX as the already\nforwarded architectural rs1 plus the decoded immediate. Drive every MEM\naddress consumer from that field instead of the general alu_result:\nio_dmemAddr, byte-lane masks, load extraction position, memory-alignment\nchecks, and the aligned RVFI memory address. Keep alu_result as the normal\nALU/branch-link/M result for writeback and EX/MEM forwarding. Loads continue\nto select their extended memory data into MEM/WB.write_value, while stores\ncontinue to use the original separately forwarded rs2 value.\n\nThe dedicated address adder must bypass the ALU A/B control muxes, binary\nALU operation/result selection, jump-link override, and blocking M-result\nselection. Use full 32-bit modular rs1 + imm arithmetic, including all upper\nbits and low alignment bits; do not truncate to the FPGA RAM address width,\npre-align requests, specialize to CoreMark addresses, or change the allowed\nmemory map. A valid legal LOAD/STORE has alu_src=1 and is_auipc=0, so this\nvalue is bit-identical to its existing ALU_ADD effective address.\n\nClock effective_addr at the same EX/MEM boundary with the existing dmem\nhold enable. Retain it while stall is asserted, so every pending LOAD/STORE\npresents stable address, data and masks until acceptance. Give this field\nits own next-state assignment after the normal EX/MEM bundle assignment:\non each nonreset, nonstalled edge capture rs1 + in.imm, independently of\nex_wait, M ownership/completion, recovery, fast opcode or instruction\nvalidity. Reset may clear it. The field is meaningful only for a valid\nmemory request; M operations and bubbles need no saved effective address.\nThis prevents the new address register from inheriting the general result's\nwide bubble-clear or M-completion mux. Keep the existing EX/MEM controls,\nvalidity and all other payload next-state priorities exactly intact.\nNo new pipeline stage, bypass source, issue rule or memory transaction is\nintroduced, and no load-use bubble is removed.\n\nUse effective_addr consistently for both successful and misaligned memory\naccesses. Preserve word/halfword alignment traps and suppress bus enables\nand destination writes on those traps. RVFI still reports the word-aligned\naddress only for an actual nontrapped access, with the same original byte\nmasks, replicated store data and raw read word. A nonmemory instruction's\nunused physical address can differ, but it must keep zero memory masks and\nnever create a read/write transaction. Preserve exact register results,\nraw RVFI source operands, predictor feedback/recovery, once-only blocking\nM requests and completions, stable held WB forwarding, nret=1 and contiguous\nchannel-0 retirement order. No multiplier/divider, ALTOPS, decoder legality,\nFPGA wrapper, memory-map or correctness-contract change is proposed.\n\nThe prediction is unchanged canonical stalled CoreMark cycles and a\n12-percent fitness gain from removing generic result steering from the\ninferred RAM address timing cone. This is a local address-generation\nmicro-optimization with one extra adder/register path, not a pipeline\nredesign. At unchanged cycles, acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax above\n77.96361 MHz and fitness above 188.76116 iterations/second. Adder duplication,\nnew register routing and a remaining redirect/ordinary-ALU path may limit\nthe gain. Run the unchanged correctness gates and measure actual canonical\nmarker cycles, CRCs and vendor median timing before claiming acceptance.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':12,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add a documented 32-bit effective_addr field to ex_mem_t. Keep general\nalu_result, raw forwarded operands, controls and all RVFI fields intact;\nretain every existing module and top-level nret=1 interface.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Compute the dedicated full-width rs1 + immediate address from the existing\npost-forwarding rs1. Register effective_addr on each unstalled EX/MEM edge\nusing a separate field assignment that overrides any irrelevant M/bubble\npayload copy; hold it under dmem backpressure. Preserve all other bundle\nassignments, validity, controls, general ALU results, branch/link behavior,\nM ownership/captured operands, producer permission and recovery priorities.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Replace every use of alu_result as an effective memory address with the\nnew effective_addr field: physical address, byte mask, load lane extraction,\nalignment detection and aligned RVFI address. Preserve alu_result captures\nand ordinary unified writeback selection. Keep request gating, trap\ncancellation, store replication, memory masks/data and held WB semantics.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'''Adapt EX/MEM packed result layouts and retain every existing assertion.\nVerify the dedicated address against independent full-width rs1 + imm for\nnear/far/RF sources, positive/negative offsets, wraparound and low alignment\nbits. Check stable address under dmem holds, exact generic ALU/link/M\nresults and absence of memory effects for invalid/nonmemory instructions.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all existing monitors and architectural assertions while adapting\nEX/MEM layout references. Exercise ALU/load/M producers used as subsequent\nload/store bases, load-to-store address/data dependencies, both bypass\nsources, x0, byte/half/word lanes and misalignment traps. Combine dmem\nholds with imem stalls, redirects and blocking M completion; verify exact\nRVFI addresses/masks/operands, contiguous order and once-only memory effects.\nRun unchanged formal/cosim and canonical stalled CoreMark/Gowin median gates.'''}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,default_flow_style=False))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Revised hypothesis written and schema validated:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
