etch skid buffer, predictor\npolicy, blocking M units and nret=1 interface.\n\nOn a consumer capture edge, the near producer is the instruction\nactually entering EX/MEM, normally the current ID/EX instruction. The\nfar producer is the instruction actually entering MEM/WB, normally the\ncurrent EX/MEM instruction. Match the offered instruction's raw rs1/rs2\nfields against those destination numbers, excluding x0, and combine\neach match with the producer's actual next-state write permission before\nregistering the choice. Derive near permission from execute's\ntrap-adjusted control and validity, and far permission from memory's\ntrap-adjusted control and validity. A misaligned JAL/JALR or memory\naccess must not claim a forwarding slot.\n…[9608 of 12680 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a97bc2f6d58fbfb6]…\nalls. The unchanged CoreMark IPC\nvalidator passes canonical CRCs, UART, bounds and marker checks with\nexactly 4,193,876 cycles for ten iterations. Median vendor timing times\nmeasured IPC implies prototype fitness 223.44, 6.68 percent above 209.45.\nThese are prototype measurements, not an accepted tournament result.\nFull CPU riscv-formal has not been run for this prototype; the shift\nmiter does not replace that gate. Complete all normal implementation\ngates without weakening them. The principal risks are selector fanout,\nfour-way mux routing and independent branch/recovery paths taking over.\n\nPredict a seven-percent fitness improvement with medium confidence and\nunchanged canonical CoreMark cycles. Acceptance requires actual fitness\ngreater than 219.0847; at 4,193,876 cycles median Fmax must exceed\n91.879594 MHz. Verify exact shift bits against an independent reference,\nrun unchanged formal/cosim and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC, UART,\nbounds and marker validation, and measure all vendor placement options.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
