{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('category')=='structural':\n  h=r['hypothesis']\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r['outcome'])\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('Notes:',r.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 Remove unrolled division from EX with a blocking iterative divider improvement\nMake DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU blocking multicycle EX operations using one\nshared radix-2 restoring divider. Capture the post-forwarding operands\nand operation exactly once, register quotient/remainder state, and\nperform one 33-bit subtract/select step per cycle for 32 iterations.\nRegister sign correction and the final result before it returns to the\nordinary EX/MEM path. This removes every real combinational / and %\noperator from the synthesized ALU, while keeping RV32I and multiplication\nat their current latency.\n\nFreeze fetch and ID/EX while the divider owns EX, but let older EX/MEM\nand MEM/WB instructions drain. Insert invalid, side-effect-free EX/MEM\nbubbles while waiting; do not repeatedly hold and reissue an older\nmemory operation. Existing dmem backpressure still holds a pending older\nmemory request, and has priority over advancing EX/MEM. Accept a divide\nonly from a valid legal instruction when EX can start it; retain a\ncompleted result until downstream accepts it. On that acceptance edge,\nrelease ID/EX and transfer the captured instruction and operands with\nits result exactly once. Captured operands must not depend on forwarding\nsources remaining live after older instructions retire. Qualify redirects\nwith a valid instruction actually advancing, and cancel divider state on\nreset. Preserve the nret=1 interface, channel-0 retirement order and all\nexisting decoder, trap, memory and RVFI behavior.\n\nImplement signed division by unsigned magnitudes, restoring quotient sign\nfrom the operand-sign XOR and remainder sign from the dividend. Preserve\ntruncation toward zero, quotient all-ones/remainder dividend on division\nby zero, and INT_MIN/-1 quotient INT_MIN/remainder zero. Keep all existing\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas bit-identical; pass their registered results\nthrough the same request/completion handshake within the existing formal\ndepth, using the established arithmetic abstraction rather than changing\nany fo\n…[48178 of 51250 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1d3a5083fc63df9b]…\na dedicated two-bit\nJALR alignment calculation, rather than feeding registered recovery\nor the conditional-branch predicate back into bypass selection.\nThe older canceled jump cannot hide an older valid same-rd writer.\nThis writer qualification is part of the control-resolution boundary,\nnot a change to the decoder's legality policy or source priority.\n\nRequire unchanged nret=1 IO, contiguous channel-0 retirement, complete\nRV32IM arithmetic and ALTOPS behavior, stable stalled memory requests,\nonce-only effects and symbolic progress. Preserve all checks, bus\nstall methodology, address bounds, canonical CoreMark CRCs, UART\nvalidation text and marker-only cycle accounting. Measure actual\nGowin median timing: at unchanged cycles, more than 98.019 MHz is\nneeded to exceed the champion by 4.6 percent. Expect eight percent\nfitness improvement from the structural cut. Main risks are the\nresulting registered-recovery-to-fetch path and remaining ALU,\nJALR-alignment or predictor paths becoming the timing limit.\n\nNotes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
