ds a pending older\nmemory request, and has priority over advancing EX/MEM. Accept a divide\nonly from a valid legal instruction when EX can start it; retain a\ncompleted result until downstream accepts it. On that acceptance edge,\nrelease ID/EX and transfer the captured instruction and operands with\nits result exactly once. Captured operands must not depend on forwarding\nsources remaining live after older instructions retire. Qualify redirects\nwith a valid instruction actually advancing, and cancel divider state on\nreset. Preserve the nret=1 interface, channel-0 retirement order and all\nexisting decoder, trap, memory and RVFI behavior.\n\nImplement signed division by unsigned magnitudes, restoring quotient sign\nfr\n…[14811 of 17883 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3cd5bc3c69f2791f]…\nstorical tracing found 135,320 immediate load-to-branch pairs and\nabout 62,551 next-PC errors in the same CoreMark instruction stream.\nThose are model counts, not measured penalties for this champion;\nother load consumers, false dependencies, bus overlap and altered\ntraining latency also affect cycles. Use six percent more bracketed\ncycles, about 4,648,015, as a provisional engineering budget. At that\ncount, beating the champion by 4.6 percent requires median Fmax above\n62.97 MHz. A 70 MHz result would score about 150.60 iterations/second,\na 16.28 percent gain. Predict a 16 percent fitness improvement with\nmedium confidence. The main risks are the X-result-to-O bypass becoming\nthe next timing limit, extra load stalls exceeding the budget, and\nplacement cost of the new registers. The actual hypothesis succeeds\nonly if (new_Fmax / 56.790) / (new_cycles / 4384920) exceeds 1.046.\nPreserve all decoder defaults, traps, arithmetic, memory bounds,\ncanonical CRCs, marker timing and unchanged formal/cosim/eval gates.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
