{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20260930-002-r7s1',\n'title':'Split execute effects into registered memory-address and recovery lanes',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 180.46 iterations/second at 74.535 MHz and\n4,130,235 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst Gowin path runs from\nMEM/WB write_value through EX and the general ALU into the data-memory\naddress input: 19.124 ns across 13 cells. Registered bypass choices\nhave already removed producer matching and priority from EX, but\nmemory addresses still traverse general operand/result muxes, while\nresolved next-PC feedback drives fetch and predictor memories directly.\nEarlier structural fetch/operand-stage experiments either added cycles\nwithout raising Fmax or failed stalled cosim. Replacing register storage\nalso left the same execution-to-memory limit. Preserve those lessons by\nseparating execute's externally applied effects at its existing output\nboundary, rather than adding a general operand stage.\n\nScratch measurements against the unchanged wrappers show why this must\nbe one complete output-lane split. A dedicated address alone reached\nonly 71.281 MHz median and exposed forwarding-to-frontend feedback;\nregistered recovery alone reached only 69.288 MHz and retained the ALU\npath into dmem. Separating and registering both effect lanes reached\n82.878 MHz median, with the worst path now ending at an integer-result\nregister instead of either memory endpoint. This proposal selects that\nsingle execution-output topology, not either incomplete prototype.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Restructure execute's output into three parallel lanes at the existing\nEX/MEM edge: the established integer writeback result, a dedicated\nload/store effective address, and a registered frontend-recovery event.\nMemory and frontend effects consume the latter two registered lanes;\nthey no longer consume the general ALU result or live EX decisions.\nFast arithmetic retains its existing latency and bypass structure.\nThere is no new ordinary execution stage, issue bubble or load-use\npenalty. Recovery on a next-PC mismatch moves one cycle later and must\ncancel the extra younger instruction now occupying EX. Keep nret=1,\nthe current retirement boundary, distributed-RAM RF, complete captured\nforwarding selections, unified WB result, predictor policy, fetch skid\nbuffer and blocking MUL/DIV algorithms.\n\nAdd a full-width effective_address field to ex_mem_t. Compute it as\nforwarded rs1 + registered imm, independently of alu_src, is_auipc,\nALU opcode/result selection and jump-link selection. Capture it beside\nthe existing integer alu_result on the same edge. In mem_stage use it\nfor dmem_addr, access alignment, byte mask position, load extraction\noffset and aligned RVFI mem_addr. Keep all load/ALU write_value routing\nand store-data forwarding unchanged. Preserve modulo-2\"'^32 addition,\nnegative I/S immediates and complete MMIO addresses; never narrow to\nthe benchmark memory map. Populate the field in fast and saved blocking\nmetadata consistently. Existing EX/MEM holds retain the entire request,\nand invalid/M tokens never acquire memory effects.\n\nKeep branch conditions, alignment traps, resolved pc_next and the two\nparallel predicted-successor comparisons in EX. Their valid advancing\nmismatch produces a computed recovery event, but register redirect,\nredirect_target and predictor_update_t before exposing them to IF.\nCapture predictor feedback for each actual EX advancement exactly once;\nnonadvancing cycles produce update.valid=0. Keep conditional training,\nunconditional entries, target tagging and default non-control\ninvalidation behavior unchanged. Registered table feedback has one\ncycle more latency. PC-relative and JALR arithmetic and the canonical\nmisalignment recovery successor remain exact.\n\nAn asserted pending registered recovery belongs to the older EX/MEM\ninstruction. Suppress younger EX advancement, predictor updates,\nproducer permission and all MUL/DIV requests while it is pending.\nFor the prototype, qualify is_mul/is_div, forward_write and advancing\nwith !redirect. On recovery, let the owning older instruction advance\nthrough MEM/WB, squash the current younger EX token into an EX/MEM\nbubble, and use the existing redirect inputs to flush ID/EX and the\nfetch queue and set the fetch PC. No younger store may reach memory,\nno younger M request may start, and no canceled instruction may train\nor retire. Correctly predicted control transfers continue streaming.\n\nRecovery must also work when a stale prediction belongs to a load or\nstore rather than a branch. Preserve EX/MEM on dmem stall BEFORE the\nrecovery bubble case, and retain the pending redirect bit until that\nolder request can advance. One concrete register rule is: reset clears\nredirect; otherwise update it from computed_redirect only when !stall.\nHold the older address/data/masks through every wait and accept its\nmemory effect exactly once. IF may receive the same pending recovery\nwhile blocked, but must not consume younger tokens; the eventual\nunblocked edge must annul the held ID/EX token. Never discard the\nolder memory instruction to apply recovery. Predictor update.valid is\na one-shot even while redirect is retained, so a prolonged memory stall\ndoes not repeatedly train a counter. Pending payload needs no new reset\nbeyond validity/ownership cancellation. Preserve existing MEM/WB data\nretention for forwarding during holds.\n\nThe concrete EX/MEM priority is reset, dmem hold, pending recovery\nbubble, registered M completion, unfinished-M bubble, ordinary fast\ntoken. Computed recovery requires in.valid && !redirect && !reset &&\n!stall && !ex_wait. Capture the resolved target and complete predictor\nupdate in registers; derive returned redirect only from its pending\nregister. The decoded M producer already retains its instruction and\nforwarded operands through ownership. Since recovery suppresses the\nextra younger EX token before request acceptance, no new M cancellation\nprotocol is needed. Preserve every existing RV32M and ALTOPS formula,\nillegal/default decoder rule, alignment trap, RVFI operand and memory\nfield, contiguous channel-0 retirement and stable bus handshake.\n\nA scratch prototype of exactly this topology, including older-memory\nhold priority and retained recovery, used the unchanged vendor flow,\nFPGA wrapper and timed stall generator. Gowin placement options 0/1/2\nreached 78.992/82.878/82.878 MHz, giving 82.878 MHz median. Option 0\nreports 3,484 logic cells, 1,182 registers, 32 RAM16s, 7 BSRAMs and\n2 DSPs. Its worst path is 17.954 ns across 13 cells from WB write_value\nto the EX/MEM integer-result register, rather than dmem or predictor\nmemory. All eight selftest/random1/random2/random3 trace-cosim cases\npassed, including combined imem/dmem stalls. The unchanged\nrun_coremark_ipc validator passed canonical CRCs, UART, bounds and\nmarkers: 10 iterations in 4,193,876 bracketed cycles, 1.54 percent more\nthan the champion. Timing times IPC predicts approximately 197.62\niterations/second, 9.51 percent above 180.46. These are scratch\nmeasurements, not an accepted tournament result.\n\nAn isolated copy of the unchanged formal runner, wrapper, checks and\nupstream framework has passed 52 of 53 checks on the preceding\nprototype; register consistency remains in progress at this writing.\nThat prototype differs only in recovery/dmem-hold priority, which the\nformal wrapper'\"'s tied-high ready signals cannot exercise. This is not\na full formal pass. Complete all ordinary gates on the final RTL and\nadd directed stale-prediction plus memory-backpressure coverage during\nimplementation without modifying any contract file or weakening tests.\nThe main risks are recovery age/hold mistakes, a slow register proof,\nand placement variation. Acceptance requires (new_Fmax / 74.535) /\n(new_cycles / 4130235) > 1.046; at the measured cycle count the median\nmust exceed 79.165 MHz. Predict 9 percent with medium confidence.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':9,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add a separate full-width effective_address to ex_mem_t, retaining\ninteger results and all architectural/RVFI metadata. Keep nret and\nall top-level interfaces unchanged.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Implement the parallel registered address and recovery lanes.\nCompute address directly from forwarded rs1+imm; register mismatch,\nresolved target and complete one-shot predictor feedback. Suppress\nyounger EX effects during pending recovery and squash its EX/MEM\ntransfer, while retaining the older request first on dmem holds.\nHold pending recovery until the older token can advance. Preserve\nblocking M ownership, source captures, writeback and trap behavior.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Use the dedicated effective_address for the bus address, alignment,\nbyte position, load offset and RVFI memory address. Preserve existing\nwriteback selection, trap-adjusted far permission, store data/masks,\nstable stalled requests and once-only memory effects.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'''Update EX/MEM packing and redirect expectations to the registered\nevent while preserving existing assertions. Check independent\naddress/result fields, one-shot training, canceled younger fast/M\ninstructions, completed M consumers, reset and recovery across long\ndmem holds. Verify the older token advances before the younger bubble.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve existing tests and cover the extra recovery slot, wrong-path\nstores and M operations, repeated redirects, both-source forwarding,\nload-use, x0, alignment traps and full-width I/S/MMIO addresses with\ncombined bus stalls. Train a PC as a transfer, then supply a load/store\nat that PC to create a stale prediction; stall its memory request\nthrough recovery and assert stable payload, exact single acceptance\nand retirement, correct RVFI memory/operands and eventual younger\nsquash. Run unchanged formal, cosim, canonical CoreMark and median\nvendor timing before accepting the implementation.'''}]\n}\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r7s1.yaml')\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,default_flow_style=False,allow_unicode=True,width=88))\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20260930-002-r7s1' and d['category']=='structural'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
