{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_33", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r10s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20260930-003-r10s2\ntitle: \\\"Prepare successor prediction checks before execute-stage branch resolution\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Registered recovery is still a timing constraint after the champion's\n  operand and shifter improvements. The r9s2 experiment's worst path ran\n  from MEM/WB forwarded data to EX redirect through 31 cells; r9s1 likewise\n  exposed forwarding-to-redirect timing. The existing predictor compares\n  a forwarded JALR base plus its immediate with the predicted next PC in\n  EX. PC-relative target addition/comparison and target-alignment checks\n  also remain in EX. Move prediction verification preparation before\n  ID/EX, retaining the predictor policy and fetch topology, to shorten\n  resolution without adding recovery cycles. A canonical CoreMark ISS\n  profile found only 1,194 ideal return-target errors attributable to\n  changing callers, making a return stack alone insufficient for the\n  required 4.6 percent fitness gain; this proposal targets Fmax instead.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the current 64-entry tagged two-bit next-PC predictor and one-entry\n  fall-through fetch skid unchanged. Prepare the exact successor-check\n  information in ID and register it with its instruction. This is one\n  predictor recovery optimization, with no new predictor algorithm,\n  instruction replay, speculative execution policy, or pipeline stage.\n\n  Compute and capture static_control_target = PC + decoded immediate,\n  prediction_mismatch_sequential = predicted_next_pc \"'!= PC+4, and\n  prediction_mismatch_static = predicted_next_pc != static_control_target\n  in ID/EX. Branches and JAL use that registered static target for actual\n  resolution and feedback, and their registered mismatch bit for recovery.\n  Non-control, illegal and misaligned instructions retain sequential\n  recovery using the registered sequential mismatch. EX still selects the\n  one-bit mismatch with the actual forwarded branch/jump outcome and\n  registers recovery at its existing EX/MEM ownership boundary.\n\n  For JALR, precompute two full 32-bit modular inverse candidates in ID:\n  expected_rs1_a = predicted_next_pc - immediate and expected_rs1_b =\n  predicted_next_pc + 1 - immediate. EX compares its forwarded rs1 in\n  parallel against these two registered values. A target prediction\n  matches exactly when predicted_next_pc[0] is zero and either equality\n  holds. This is the exact equivalence for RV32 JALR bit-zero clearing:\n  ((rs1 + imm) & 0xfffffffe) == predicted_next_pc. It handles positive and\n  negative immediates, odd bases and modulo-2''^32 wrap; the two candidate\n  values must not be confused with assumptions about base parity.\n  Preserve the existing rs1+imm adder in parallel to compute the actual\n  aligned redirect target and architectural RVFI pc_next. Its output no\n  longer feeds the wide prediction equality comparator, removing the\n  serial forwarded-base -> carry chain -> equality-reduction path.\n\n  Keep static target alignment and actual taken qualification correct.\n  A misaligned taken branch/JAL/JALR must still trap, suppress jump link\n  writes, train no target, and recover to PC+4. Untaken conditional branches\n  must not trap just because their encoded target is misaligned. A stale\n  prediction for a non-control or illegal instruction must also recover.\n  Recovery/training qualifiers, target ownership and priorities remain\n  exactly as in the champion: one update per advancing valid instruction,\n  retained older recovery through dmem stalls, and annulled younger stores\n  and M requests. No speculation or prediction correctness is presumed.\n\n  Capture every new field only when the original ID/EX payload captures;\n  retain it through load-use holds, dmem stalls and blocking M execution.\n  A skid token uses its captured predicted_next_pc, never a new table lookup.\n  Narrow validity/side-effect kills still cancel wrong-path instructions.\n  Preserve all nret=1 ports, decode legality, real/ALTOPS M arithmetic,\n  memory protocol, contiguous RVFI order and bounded forward progress.\n\n  The intended CoreMark cycle count is unchanged from 4,193,876 under the\n  standard independent bus stalls. The success condition is measured\n  median Gowin Fmax above 98.018568 MHz if cycles are identical, giving\n  fitness more than 4.6 percent above 223.44. Expect roughly eight percent\n  fitness improvement from shortening recovery and static alignment\n  paths. Main risks are a new ID immediate-add/subtract/comparison path\n  becoming critical and extra ID/EX registers affecting placement; this\n  estimate needs implementation and vendor median verification. Keep all\n  formal/cosim, canonical CRCs, validation literal, marker-only timing and\n  out-of-bounds gates intact; do not alter any evaluator or contract file.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Extend id_ex_t with static_control_target, registered sequential\n      and static prediction mismatch bits, and the two full-width JALR\n      inverse-base candidates. Preserve predicted_next_pc and RVFI metadata.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Prepare static target, both static successor mismatch bits and\n      modular JALR inverse candidates from the offered token and decoded\n      immediate. Capture them on the existing payload enable, with exact\n      hold/flush ownership and no extra pipeline stage or live table lookup.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the registered static target for branch/JAL resolution and\n      registered successor mismatch bits for recovery. Replace JALR\n      sum-to-predicted-PC comparison with parallel forwarded-rs1 equality\n      checks against the registered inverse candidates, guarded by the\n      predicted PC low bit. Compute actual JALR target in parallel and\n      preserve alignment traps, link writes, once-only table feedback,\n      EX/MEM recovery retention and younger-effect cancellation.\n  - file: test/test_ex_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Update ID/EX packing while preserving existing assertions. Test\n      inverse checks against actual JALR addition/bit-zero clearing for\n      random bases/immediates, odd/even predictions, wraparound and negative\n      offsets, plus static branch/JAL mismatch, taken/untaken misalignment,\n      stale ordinary/illegal predictions, holds and one-shot recovery.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update token-layout monitors and retain all current checks. Cover\n      ALU/load-forwarded JALR, positive/negative offsets, link consumers,\n      aligned and misaligned target outcomes, predicted-sequential aliases,\n      held skid tokens and recovery coincident with independent bus stalls\n      and blocking M operations. Assert exact RVFI successors, sources,\n      traps, link results and order, once-only memory effects and progress.\n'\"''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nobj=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(obj,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
