{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_62", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r12s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20260930-003-r12s2\ntitle: \"Banked RAM16 next-PC predictor with independent training reads\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 223.44 at 93.708 MHz with 4,193,876 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. Its r9s0 prototype reported a predictor critical path\n  for the two median placement options. Recent attempts to move successor\n  comparison into decode or behind EX/MEM lowered Fmax without saving\n  cycles. The existing 64-entry predictor already removes most taken\n  transfer bubbles, so improving its physical storage implementation is\n  a more focused opportunity than adding prediction-verification logic.\n\n  The predictor's tags and counters have two asynchronous read addresses:\n  fetch lookup and registered feedback training. Its payload arrays have\n  no explicit distributed RAM banking, while the accepted register-file\n  change demonstrated the value of 16-word RAM16 arrays on this FPGA.\n  The r9s2 experiment also showed that bank-local tag/direction decisions\n  outperform selecting a wide tag before comparing it; that experiment\n  combined this with replay and a registered FIFO. Isolate the predictor\n  storage change on the current champion, preserving its fall-through\n  fetch topology and registered final recovery decision.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the single 64-entry next-PC table in if_stage.sv with four\n  constant-index, 16-entry distributed RAM banks. Keep the exact existing\n  prediction algorithm, capacity and indexing: PC[7:6] selects the bank,\n  PC[5:2] selects its row, PC[31:8] is the complete 24-bit tag, and each\n  entry stores target[31:2], an unconditional bit and a saturating two-bit\n  direction counter. This is one physical predictor-storage hypothesis.\n\n  Put payload arrays inside constant generate banks with explicit\n  syn_ramstyle = \"distributed_ram\" attributes, synchronous writes and\n  asynchronous reads. Give tags and counters two coherent copies per\n  bank, one read only at the fetch row and one read only at the update\n  row. Both copies receive identical bank-local writes.\n…[2372 of 5444 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c220db462f7cdd3b]…\nl/cosim gates and canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs, UART validation,\n  address bounds and marker-only cycle accounting; do not weaken checks.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Bank only the existing predictor into four 16-entry RAM16 payload\n      arrays, explicitly duplicate fetch/training tag and counter views,\n      resolve full-tag hits and prediction direction within each bank,\n      and combine masked target words. Preserve table semantics, fetch\n      acceptance/skid behavior, update timing and recovery topology.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Keep all existing assertions. Add checks that all four banks retain\n      distinct entries with the same row, full-tag replacement affects\n      only its selected bank, and simultaneous fetch/training at equal\n      and different rows preserves pre-edge predictions and saturation.\n      Exercise invalidation, reset and queued predictions during stalls.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
