{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_41", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r13s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20260930-003-r13s2\ntitle: \\\"Validate predicted successors with parallel carry consistency\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 223.44 at 93.708 MHz over 4,193,876 marker-bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. Prior predictor experiments expose forwarded operands\n  through target addition and successor equality into registered recovery.\n  Moving equality into decode or after EX/MEM reduced Fmax; cached operand\n  certificates twice exceeded the formal timeout. Preserve the current\n  prediction table, metadata and recovery boundaries and replace the\n  arithmetic-dependent verification itself with an exact local relation.\n\n  Fresh scratch probes of eight-, twelve- and sixteen-bit folded BTB tags\n  measured median Gowin Fmax of 91.173, 78.279 and 93.681 MHz respectively.\n  They do not beat the champion and are not part of this proposal. Exact\n  carry-consistency verification avoids both wider predictor storage and\n  the correspondence proof needed for cached target certificates.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace EX's addition-then-equality successor checks with a parallel\n  carry-consistency verifier. For 32-bit operands A and B and captured\n  predicted sum P, infer each incoming carry as C = A XOR B XOR P.\n  Derive the outgoing carry independently at each bit as\n  G = (A AND B) OR ((A XOR B) AND NOT P).\n  The prediction is exactly A+B modulo 2\"'^32 if and only if\n  C XOR {G[30:0], 1'\"'b0} is zero. Bit zero checks the required initial\n  carry of zero; every higher bit checks one adjacent carry relation.\n  Do not check carry out of bit 31, since RV32 address addition wraps.\n  Compute all local relations in parallel followed by a balanced OR\n  reduction, instead of passing a completed carry-propagated sum into\n  a wide equality comparator. Use a helper function or explicit wires\n  compatible with both Gowin and the existing Yosys formal frontend.\n\n  Use this exact check with A=in.pc and B=4 for the sequential successor,\n  and A=in.pc and B=in.imm for PC-relative branch/JAL targets. For JALR\n  use A=the actual forwarded rs1 and B=in.imm, accounting exactly for\n  architectural clearing of the sum's bit zero. Set mismatch bit zero\n  to P[0], independently compute the true carry into bit one as\n  A[0] AND B[0], and compare it with inferred C[1]. Bits 2 through 31\n  use the same adjacent local relations as above. Merely masking the\n  ordinary verifier's bit zero is incorrect: it would infer the wrong\n  carry into bit one when the unclipped sum is odd. No cached operand,\n  inverse signature, predicted control type, shortened address, or\n  conservative extra recovery is needed.\n\n  Keep the existing actual target adders, forwarded branch comparison,\n  target-alignment traps, jump link value, resolved_taken selection,\n  computed_redirect_target, and architectural RVFI pc_next. Replace\n  only mismatch_sequential and mismatch_target. Select the JALR check\n  using the decoded is_jalr control, and select the final mismatch using\n  the actual resolved_taken exactly as today. Verification continues\n  for every valid advancing instruction, including ordinary instructions,\n  illegal instructions, alignment faults and completed M operations\n  carrying stale predictions. Invalid tokens never redirect. The exact\n  Boolean relation must produce precisely the old mismatch result for\n  every operand and captured prediction, preserving CoreMark cycles.\n\n  Preserve the original 64-entry full-tag BTB, direction counters,\n  registered once-per-advance predictor feedback, accepted-fetch and\n  fall-through skid behavior, all prediction metadata and stage widths.\n  Keep registered final recovery owned by EX/MEM and its target stable\n  through dmem stalls; recovery overrides bus readiness and queue fullness\n  and squashes every younger store and M request. Preserve decoder\n  validation, alignment gating, M arithmetic/ALTOPS, full bus handshakes,\n  nret=1 IO, exact RVFI retirement order and forward progress. Change no\n  evaluation files or formal assumptions.\n\n  Prove the helper equivalent to full 32-bit modular addition equality\n  for both ordinary and bit-zero-cleared modes using a standalone\n  combinational SAT miter. Exercise long carry chains, alternating bits,\n  negative offsets, overflow, all-zero/all-one words, arbitrary predicted\n  bits, odd/even equivalent JALR bases and mismatches at every bit. Retain\n  all existing EX and pipeline assertions. Require the unchanged full\n  formal/cosim gates, canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs and validation\n  literal, marker-only cycles, bounds and median Gowin timing.\n\n  Expect six percent fitness improvement, with low confidence pending\n  measurement. At unchanged 4,193,876 cycles, acceptance needs median\n  Fmax above approximately 98.02 MHz. The potential gain comes solely\n  from shorter exact prediction verification with no additional recovery\n  or fetch latency. Risks are local-check reduction routing, duplication\n  of verification logic alongside actual target adders, and the existing\n  forwarding-permission path remaining dominant. Prototype measurements,\n  if recorded here, do not constitute a full formal or tournament pass.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement exact parallel carry-consistency verification and use it\n      for sequential, PC-relative and bit-zero-cleared JALR mismatch\n      checks. Preserve full target arithmetic, architectural successors,\n      alignment traps, actual outcomes, registered recovery and feedback,\n      M ownership, forwarding and all advancement/hold/squash priorities.\n  - file: test/test_ex_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing assertions and add meaningful exact-successor\n      verification vectors: long carries, wraparound, negative immediates,\n      every predicted bit error, odd/even JALR sums and bit-one carry from\n      bit zero. Compare recovery decisions/targets with full-width sums\n      under advancement, bubble, dmem hold and M completion conditions.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain pipeline checks and exercise forwarded JALR operands, changing\n      indirect targets, taken/not-taken loops, stale ordinary/illegal/M\n      predictions, full-skid recovery and both bus stalls. Check exact RVFI\n      PCs/order/traps/link writes and no younger register/store/M effects.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r13s2.yaml'\nh=yaml.safe_load(open(p))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
