{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_64", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r14s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Compact associative next-PC prediction with local balanced training'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 223.44 at 93.708 MHz over 4,193,876 bracketed\nCoreMark cycles. Its 64-entry direct-mapped predictor suffers index\nconflicts, but larger lookup networks can dominate the fetch clock.\nPrior RAM banking and folded tags did not beat the champion, while\ncached successor certificates twice timed out in formal. Fresh scratch\nprobes rejected a 64-entry register expansion at 65.406 MHz and a\n16-entry associative table at 86.119 MHz; the latter's worst path was\nfeedback-PC comparison through global hit selection into direction\ntraining, rather than instruction execution.\n\nAn architectural trace of canonical RV32IM -O3 CoreMark contains\n592,690 control transfers at 237 static sites. Immediate-training\nmodels give 62,551 successor errors for the original 64-entry table,\n94,560 for 16 associative entries, and 61,289 for 24 associative entries\nwith FIFO replacement. Thus 24 entries retain useful accuracy with\nsubstantially less register lookup state than a 64-entry expansion.\nThese are trace-model results, not speculative RTL cycle measurements.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Replace the current 64-entry direct-mapped next-PC predictor with a\n24-entry fully associative register table using exact PC[31:2] tags,\naligned 30-bit targets, unconditional flags, valid bits and two-bit\nsaturating direction counters. Use insertion-order FIFO replacement:\na five-bit victim pointer resets to zero and advances modulo 24 only\non allocation of a previously absent PC; matching updates do not move\nit. No PC-specific or CoreMark-specific specialization is allowed.\n\nConstant-index entries independently determine tag match and direction\nbefore masking their target. OR masked targets through balanced groups\nof four, then a balanced final reduction. Sequential PC+4 is selected\nwhen no entry predicts taken. All 30 tag bits must participate, with\nno hashes, shortened address ranges or speculative certificate checks.\nKeep at most one valid entry per complete PC. Allocate an absent PC\nonly in the current victim, update matching controls in place, and\ninvalidate only full-PC matches on non-control or invalid feedback.\nOnly valid bits and the victim pointer reset; payload registers do not.\nConfirm the vendor synthesizes register entries, not indexed RAM reads.\n\nMake exact lookup and training tag equality explicit parallel small\ncomparisons followed by a balanced AND reduction. Preserve four-bit\ncomparison groups where needed to prevent synthesis from replacing the\nbalanced network with a long arithmetic comparison chain. Keep local\nmatching-entry direction training independent of global any-hit and\nvictim selection. Global miss selection controls only new allocation;\nexisting counters update directly from their local full-tag match.\nImplement saturating counter transitions with Boolean equations rather\nthan increment/decrement and terminal-count muxes. For counter (H,L),\ntaken produces (H OR L, H OR NOT L), while not-taken produces\n(H AND L, H AND NOT L). A newly allocated entry gets 10 for taken and\n01 for not-taken. Targets and unconditional flags remain coherent with\nthe same entry identity. Never duplicate a PC when training and\nreplacement interact, and never invalidate a nonmatching victim.\n\nLookup uses the pre-edge table during simultaneous training without\nfeedback bypass. Capture the exact predicted_next_pc actually used to\nadvance fetch in the original token and skid metadata. Preserve the\nfall-through frontend, fetch acceptance, fill/drain, all bus stalls,\nregistered once-per-advance EX feedback, and EX/MEM-owned registered\nrecovery with retained target across dmem holds. Keep existing full-width\narchitectural successor verification for every valid instruction,\nincluding stale predictions on ordinary, illegal and M instructions;\nkeep younger store/M-request squash and target alignment traps. Table\npredictions remain hints verified by EX, and never establish legality.\nDo not change pipeline depth, arithmetic, nret=1 IO, RVFI order,\ndecoder validation or any formal/evaluation assumption.\n\nExpect six percent fitness improvement with low confidence from a\nsmaller associative lookup and shorter local training, while reducing\ndirect-map conflicts enough to retain approximately the original cycle\ncount. The trace model does not account for feedback latency, wrong-path\nfetches or bus-stall overlap; require measured bracketed CoreMark cycles.\nAt unchanged cycles acceptance requires median Fmax above 98.02 MHz.\nMain risks are tag fanout, replacement control, placement and delayed\ntraining increasing misses. Require unchanged full formal/cosim gates,\ncanonical stalled CoreMark CRCs and validation literal, bounded memory,\nmarker-only timing and every Gowin placement option. Scratch probes do\nnot constitute a tournament correctness or improvement result.\n'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Implement the 24-entry exact-tag associative register predictor,\nFIFO replacement, balanced local comparison and target selection,\nand Boolean local counter training. Preserve captured predictions,\noriginal fetch/skid acceptance and recovery behavior.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'''Preserve all existing assertions; adapt expectations tied specifically\nto direct-map eviction to associative retention and FIFO eviction.\nAdd full-tag uniqueness, 24-entry replacement wrap, repeated-PC\ntraining, matching-only invalidation, counter transitions, simultaneous\nlookup/training and queued pre-edge metadata checks against a model.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all pipeline assertions and check control-rich loops with\nmore than 24 distinct PCs, changing JALR targets, stale code, alignment\nfaults, back-to-back predictions and full-skid recovery under both\nbus stalls. Assert exact retirement PCs/order and no younger effects.\n'''}]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=76,default_flow_style=False))\nprint('Revised complete hypothesis to the compact associative design.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
