{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_68", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r14s2.yaml')\nd={'id':'hyp-20260930-003-r14s2','title':'Verify predicted control targets with short parallel carry segments','category':'predictor','motivation':'''The champion scores 223.44 at 93.708 MHz over 4,193,876 bracketed\nCoreMark cycles. Its prediction recovery includes forwarded JALR\noperands through a 32-bit target adder and successor equality. Moving\nverification into decode or behind EX/MEM reduced Fmax, and cached\noperand certificates twice timed out in formal. The prior bitwise\ncarry-consistency verifier passed correctness but scored 220.65;\nits fine-grained Boolean network did not improve median timing.\n\nFresh isolated predictor-storage probes also rejected expanding the\n64-entry table into registers and reducing it to a compact associative\ntable. Preserve the proven predictor and instead test a different exact\nverification mapping: several short FPGA carry segments in parallel,\nwith no full-word carry propagation feeding target equality.\n''','hypothesis':'''Replace only EX's mismatch_target calculation with an exact parallel\nfour-bit segmented verifier. Retain the ordinary sequential-PC equality,\nfull architectural target adders, branch comparisons and registered\nfinal recovery. The verifier uses the captured predicted_next_pc itself\nto infer segment input carries, then checks each short sum and the\nconsistency of adjacent segments. This is a predictor verification\nchange, with no prediction table or pipeline topology change.\n\nFor A, B and predicted sum P, infer the per-bit incoming carry vector\nC = A XOR B XOR P. Split the words into eight four-bit segments. Segment\nj computes a five-bit S_j = {0,A[4j+:4]} + {0,B[4j+:4]} + c_j, with\nc_0=0 and c_j=C[4j] for j>0. A segment reports mismatch if its low four\nsum bits differ from P[4j+:4]. For j<7 also report mismatch if its carry\nout S_j[4] differs from C[4j+4]. OR all segment mismatches through a\nbalanced reduction. Ignore carry out of the highest segment, since\nRV32 addition wraps modulo 2\"'^32. Each segment is independent and has\nat most a short four-bit carry chain; no segment'\"'s computed carry feeds\nanother segment's adder. Prefer explicit fixed generate blocks or an\nunrolled compatible helper so Gowin can infer its short native carry\nstructures, rather than a 32-bit adder followed by equality.\n\nFor branch/JAL use A=in.pc, B=in.imm and P=in.predicted_next_pc.\nFor JALR use the actual forwarded rs1 and decoded immediate. Because\nJALR clears sum bit zero, reconstruct an unchecked raw predicted sum\nP_raw={predicted_next_pc[31:1], rs1[0] XOR imm[0]}. Run the same segmented\nverifier on A=rs1, B=imm and P_raw, and independently mismatch when\npredicted_next_pc[0] is one. This restores the true raw sum bit zero\nand therefore the correct carry into bit one. Simply masking an\nordinary verification error at bit zero is incorrect. Keep exact full\n32-bit equivalence for both modes, including negative offsets, odd\nJALR bases and address wraparound. No conservative extra recovery is\nallowed; the mismatch must exactly equal the existing comparison.\n\nKeep actual branch/JAL/JALR target calculation, bit-zero clearing,\nmisalignment traps, jump link writes, resolved_taken selection,\ncomputed_redirect_target and architectural RVFI pc_next unchanged.\nVerification still applies to all valid advancing instructions through\nthe original outcome-select logic, including stale predictions on\nordinary, illegal, misaligned and completed M instructions. Invalid\nbubbles must never redirect. Do not add certificate state, a decoded\npredicted kind, a training dependency or an extra stage.\n\nPreserve the original 64-entry full-tag predictor and counter policy,\npre-edge lookup, fall-through skid acceptance and captured prediction\nmetadata through holds. Retain once-only registered training and\nEX/MEM-owned recovery with a stable target across dmem holds, priority\nover queue fullness and imem readiness, and squash of every younger\nstore and M request. Preserve arithmetic/ALTOPS, all bus handshakes,\nnret=1 IO, decoder validation, RVFI order and forward progress.\n\nProve the helper against ordinary modular sum equality and bit-zero-\ncleared JALR sum equality with a standalone combinational SAT miter.\nRetain existing tests and exercise every segment boundary, long carries,\noverflow, negative offsets, all-zero/all-one operands, arbitrary\npredicted-bit errors and odd/even equivalent JALR bases. Require all\nunchanged formal/cosim gates and canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs,\nvalidation literal, bounds, marker-only cycles and median Gowin timing.\n\nExpect six percent fitness improvement with low confidence through\nshorter predictor verification, at exactly unchanged CoreMark cycles.\nAcceptance needs median Fmax above approximately 98.02 MHz. Unlike the\nprior bitwise verifier, this proposal deliberately retains short local\narithmetic carries and leaves the sequential comparator untouched.\nRisks are short-adder mapping, reduction routing, duplicated logic\nbeside the actual target adders and another execute path remaining\ndominant. Scratch timing is evidence about mapping, not a formal or\ntournament improvement claim.\n''','expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},'changes':[{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Implement exact four-bit segmented target verification for PC-relative\nand bit-zero-cleared JALR successors. Preserve sequential equality,\nactual targets/traps, full successor metadata, outcome selection,\nregistered recovery/training, M ownership and advancement priorities.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'''Retain all existing assertions and add exact modular-successor checks\ncovering every segment boundary, carries and wraparound, negative\nimmediates, predicted-bit errors and JALR bit-zero/bit-one behavior.\nExercise invalid tokens, dmem holds and arithmetic completion.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain full-core assertions. Check forwarded JALR sources, changing\ntargets, taken/untaken loops, stale ordinary/illegal/M predictions,\nfull-skid recovery and both bus stalls, with exact RVFI successors,\nretirement order and no younger register/store/M effects.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=76))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised exact-verification hypothesis written and schema-valid.')\nfor w in [4,8]:\n probe=Path(f'/srv/hwebench/tmp/e2005e29/80f04ad5/seg{w}_probe')\n s=(probe/'rtl/ex_stage.sv').read_text();a=s.index('  function automatic target_mismatch;');b=s.index('  endfunction',a)+len('  endfunction')\n verifier='module verify(input [31:0] a,b,p, output ok);\\\\n'+s[a:b]+'''\\\\nwire [31:0] actual_sum=a+b;\nwire ordinary_ok = target_mismatch(a,b,p) == (actual_sum \"'!= p);\nwire jalr_bad = p[0] || target_mismatch(a,b,{p[31:1],a[0]''^b[0]});\nwire jalr_ok = jalr_bad == ({actual_sum[31:1],1'\"'b0} \"'!= p);\nassign ok = ordinary_ok && jalr_ok;\nendmodule\n'\"'''\n (probe/'verify.sv').write_text(verifier)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
