{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg --files -g '*AGENTS*' -g '*notes*' -g '*.rpt' -g '*.json' -g 'run.py' -g '*trace*' -g '*predict*'; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id']=='hyp-20260929-001-r4s0':\n  h=d['hypothesis']; print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print({k:d.get(k) for k in ('outcome','fmax_mhz','cycles','critical_path')})\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"bench/programs/gen_random_trace.py\nschemas/hypothesis.schema.json\nschemas/eval_result.schema.json\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/html/files/release_notes-txt.html\nInsert one unconditional register boundary on the predictor_update_t\nfeedback inside if_stage, immediately before all predictor table update\nbookkeeping. EX continues to generate exactly its existing update and\nredirect signals. Capture the update payload every nonreset clock,\nindependently of fetch acceptance, stalls or update.valid; capture its\nvalid bit every clock and clear that bit on reset. Payload registers\nneed no reset. The table consumes the previous cycle's registered update,\nnever the live EX feedback. This stage accepts one update each cycle and\napplies it once on the following edge; it must neither hold and replay a\nvalid update nor suppress a pending update because fetch or EX is now\nstalled. Reset cancels the pending update and clears table validity.\n\nDerive update_index, update tag comparison, matching-entry invalidation,\ntarget/tag/unconditional writes and saturating counter decisions solely\nfrom the registered feedback and current table contents. Compute the\nupdate hit when applying the update, rather than registering a hit from\nits capture cycle: consecutive allocations and invalidations at the\nsame index must observe the preceding committed update. Retain all 64\nentries, full PC tags, target widths, two-bit direction counters,\ninitial counter states, and matching-only noncontrol invalidation.\nPreserve asynchronous PC-only prediction and synchronous RAM writes,\nwith no training bypass into lookup and no payload-memory resets.\n\nKeep redirect and redirect_target on their current immediate EX-to-PC\npath, with recovery overriding imem stalls. Preserve the combinational\nfetch/decode boundary, captured predicted_next_pc, ordinary instruction\nlatency, all hazard and memory handshake priorities, and registered\nblocking MUL/DIV behavior. The only behavioral difference is that table\nlearning becomes visible one cycle later; a stale prediction is still\ncorrected by the existing successor comparisons and validity/control\nkills. This is a feedback-retiming micro-optimization, with no new\nprediction policy or speculative instruction execution rule.\n\nThe prediction is that splitting live EX resolution from RAM update\ncontrol raises median Gowin Fmax enough to outweigh a small training\nfreshness penalty. Expected fitness improvement is 12 percent with\nmedium confidence; neither a clock gain nor unchanged cycles is yet\nmeasured. With unchanged cycles, acceptance requires Fmax greater than\n59.403 MHz; in general Fnew/56.790 divided by Cnew/4384920 must exceed\n1.046. Immediate redirect, ordinary ALU and divider recurrence paths may\nbecome the next timing limit. Very short loops or changing JALR targets\nmay add prediction misses, so evaluate actual canonical stalled CoreMark\nmarker cycles, CRCs and median vendor Fmax rather than assuming zero cost.\nPreserve nret=1, exact RVFI operands/order/traps, decoder legality, every\narithmetic check, and all immutable evaluation files.\n\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fmax_mhz': 56.156, 'cycles': 4384920, 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -13.36, 'arrival_ns': 23.654, 'logic_cells': 16, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
