{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n        h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n        print(json.dumps({k:d.get(k) for k in ('id','title','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','critical_path')}))\n        print('MOTIVATION:',d.get('motivation',h.get('motivation','') if isinstance(h,dict) else '')[:3500])\n        if d['id'].endswith('r2s2'): print('PROPOSAL:',h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"64-entry tagged next-PC predictor with two-bit branch direction\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 13.07, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.487, \"cycles\": 4197517, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_1_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -189.85, \"arrival_ns\": 200.144, \"logic_cells\": 152, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}}\nMOTIVATION: The baseline fetches sequentially and flushes the combinational IF/ID\ninstruction whenever EX resolves a taken branch or jump. Because IF/ID\nis not registered, each such transfer loses one fetch/decode opportunity,\nrather than the two bubbles suggested by the five-stage comments.\nCoreMark repeatedly executes loop branches and call sites, making these\nbubbles a promising source of cycle savings. The measured 199.837 ns\ncritical path is through forwarding and the combinational ALU; a small\nPC-indexed predictor should improve iterations/cycle without adding\nlogic to that arithmetic path. No predictor experiments or lessons are\npresent in this fixture's history.\n\nA fresh architectural trace of the canonical -O3, RV32IM, 2K,\nten-iteration CoreMark ELF contains 2,922,016 bracketed instructions,\n529,588 conditional branches, 49,367 JALs, and 13,735 JALRs. Sequential\nprediction would mispredict 357,527 next PCs. A 64-entry tagged table\nwith this policy reduces next-PC errors to 62,551; 32 entries give\n73,619 errors and 128 entries give 58,167. Thus 64 entries capture most\nof the available benefit without doubling storage for only 4,384 fewer\nerrors. This trace model uses immediate training and no speculative\npipeline or bus timing, so it supports the sizing decision rather than\nconstituting a measured RTL speedup.\n\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"PC-indexed next-PC prediction with a short recovery control path\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 119.03, \"fmax_mhz\": 49.962, \"cycles\": 4197447, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": \n…[13227 of 16299 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:07386b1f5bd378f9]…\nt and three\nrandom-program RVFI cosim comparisons also pass with both bus stalls.\nOn the corrected scalar-token RTL, vendor placement options 0/1/2\nreport 80.111/82.513/82.513 MHz, giving a median of 82.513 MHz and\napproximately 213.28 iterations/second, 10.79 percent above 192.51.\nArea is 5,143 LUT4 and 1,684 FF, with 96 LUTRAM, seven BSRAM and two\nDSP. This is prototype evidence, not an accepted tournament result.\nThe unchanged isolated formal suite passed 52 of 53 checks; register\nconsistency remained unfinished at the local 420-second cap, with no\nreported assertion failure. This is not a full formal pass. Complete\nthat check and all ordinary implementation gates without changing\nwrappers, depths, assumptions, or any other correctness contract.\nScratch cocotb checks also pass replay during bus stalls, full upper\ntags, ready-bus precedence, replacement concurrent with old-owner\nfill, FENCE.I priority over pending discovery/fill, and 2,000 cycles\nof randomized FIFO holds, drain/refill, recovery and reset.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
